course: dictionary
lesson: 10755
title: Same word, different places
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can mean different things in different places. A urinal is a kind of toilet fixed to a wall, used by men and boys for weeing while standing up. You will find them in public and school toilets. The word also has a second meaning: a bottle that a patient in hospital uses to wee into.
explain: Use the context to decide which meaning fits. «Some modern art is a puzzle about what art even is: a shop-bought urinal signed with a fake name...» Here the urinal is an object in an art gallery, not a toilet in a school. Read the words around the word, then ask: what would fit here?
explain: Health and hygiene lessons teach you to flush and wash your hands after using a urinal. Everyday phrases like «use the urinal» and «public urinals» show the toilet meaning. Check your idea by putting it into the sentence: if it still makes sense, you are probably right.
fact: A urinal is a kind of toilet fixed to a wall, used while standing up.
fact: Urinal can also mean a bottle used in hospital for a patient to wee into.
fact: Context tells you which meaning is right: a toilet in a school, or an object in an art gallery.
fact: In 1917 Marcel Duchamp sent a shop-bought urinal to an art show, and it made people ask what art really is.
fact: Some words have more than one meaning, so always read the words around them before you decide.
exercise: In health lessons we learn to flush and wash our hands after using the urinal. Where is a urinal most likely to be? | *On a wall in a toilet | In a bedroom cupboard | Under the kitchen table | Inside a school bag
exercise: «Use the urinal» in a boys' toilet means what? | *Wee while standing up at a wall toilet | Sit down and read a book | Hang a painting on the wall | Wash the classroom floor
exercise: From «a shop-bought urinal signed with a fake name», copy the word that tells us where the urinal came from. | name | fake | signed | *shop-bought
test: «Some modern art is a puzzle about what art even is.» What does puzzle mean here? | A game with pieces to fit together | A loud noise | A type of toilet | *Something hard to understand or work out
test: A patient in hospital is given a urinal. What is it? | *A bottle to wee into | A small bed with wheels | A tall cupboard for towels | A soap for washing hands
exam: Why is the urinal in the art sentence not a toilet? | It is in a school toilet for children | It is a bed for a patient | It is a bottle used in hospital | *It is an object in an art gallery, shown as art
